The first season received favorable reviews, with a [[Metacritic]] score of 73 out of 100, based on reviews from 25 critics, making it the highest rated CW show in five years.<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow |url=https://www.metacritic.com/tv/arrow |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121009000818/http://www.metacritic.com/tv/arrow |archive-date=October 9, 2012 |access-date=November 2, 2012 |work=Metacritic}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=November 6, 2012 |title=Fall TV Results: The Season's Best & Worst New Shows |url=https://www.metacritic.com/feature/best-new-television-shows-fall-2012 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140328034649/http://www.metacritic.com/feature/best-new-television-shows-fall-2012 |archive-date=March 28, 2014 |access-date=October 26, 2013 |work=Metacritic}}</ref> Review aggregation website [[Rotten Tomatoes]] calculated an approval rating of 85%, based on 36 reviews, with an average rating of 7.47/10. The site's consensus reads, "The CW nails the target with ''Arrow'', a comic book-inspired series that benefits from cinematic action sequences, strong plotting, and intriguing characters."<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow: Season 1 (2012–2013) |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s01/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150408160908/http://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s01/ |archive-date=April 8, 2015 |access-date=January 25, 2014 |work=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref> Mary McNamara of the ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'' called the series an interesting setup with a quality look, describing Amell as "a poster boy (no doubt literally) for the [[Katniss Everdeen]] set."<ref>{{cite news |last=McNamara |first=Mary |date=October 10, 2012 |title=Review: The CW's 'Arrow' right on target with a riveting superhero |url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment/tv/showtracker/la-et-st-arrow-20121010,0,554315.story |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121211025028/http://www.latimes.com/entertainment/tv/showtracker/la-et-st-arrow-20121010,0,554315.story |archive-date=December 11, 2012 |access-date=December 11, 2012 |work=Los Angeles Times}}</ref> Brian Lowry at ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'' described the series as a "handsome but stiff surrogate for [[Batman]] that could benefit from sharper execution."<ref>{{cite news |last=Lowry |first=Brian |date=October 8, 2012 |title=''Arrow'' TV Reviews |url=https://www.variety.com/review/VE1117948503 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121015113503/http://www.variety.com/review/VE1117948503 |archive-date=October 15, 2012 |access-date=November 4, 2012 |work=Variety}}</ref> In reviewing the final episode of season one, Alasdair Wilkins of ''[[The A.V. Club]]'' gave the season as a whole a rating of B+, noting that the show "hasn't quite figured everything out yet, but it's had some standout episodes."<ref>{{cite web |last=Wilkins |first=Alasdair |date=May 15, 2012 |title=Sacrifice |url=https://www.avclub.com/articles/sacrifice,97555/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130607021641/http://www.avclub.com/articles/sacrifice,97555/ |archive-date=June 7, 2013 |access-date=October 26, 2013 |work=The A.V. Club}}</ref>

The second season received acclaim from critics for the action sequences, storytelling, performances of the cast, drama, and the portrayal of Slade Wilson.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=June 2, 2016 |title=Arrow: Season 2 Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2014/05/19/arrow-season-2-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818005657/http://www.ign.com/articles/2014/05/19/arrow-season-2-review |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |access-date=June 26, 2016 |website=IGN}}</ref> Rotten Tomatoes reported a 95% approval rating based on 12 reviews, with an average rating of 8.15/10. The site's consensus reads, "The second season of ''Arrow'' boasts more fantastic action, as well as a widening cast of intriguing, richly written characters."<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow: Season 2 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s02 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171203162739/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s02/ |archive-date=December 3, 2017 |access-date=May 11, 2018 |website=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref> [[Jeff Jensen]] of ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' gave the first half of season two a rating of B+, saying, "''Arrow'' possesses an intelligence that shines through its TV-budget production values, which aren't too shabby. The writing is adult and witty, the action is exciting, and Amell holds the center with well-cultivated ease."<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Jensen |first=Jeff |date=December 16, 2013 |title=Arrow TV Review |url=https://ew.com/article/2013/12/16/arrow-tv/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140122012119/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,20760355,00.html |archive-date=January 22, 2014 |access-date=January 25, 2014 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref> ''The A.V. Club''{{'}}s Carrie Raisler gave the first half of season two a rating of A−. She said, "''Arrow'' [has] officially established itself as one of the most satisfying shows on television. The most satisfying thing of all is that it did so by respecting its characters ... [''Arrow'' respects] the character's comic-book roots in its overarching plotlines, all while using the network-appropriate soap-opera stories to do the heavy character lifting."<ref>{{cite web |last=Raisler |first=Carrie |date=December 10, 2013 |title=Arrow became great by emphasizing characters above all else |url=https://www.avclub.com/review/arrow-became-great-by-emphasizing-characters-above-200605 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131213030409/http://www.avclub.com/review/arrow-became-great-by-emphasizing-characters-above-200605 |archive-date=December 13, 2013 |access-date=February 4, 2014 |work=The A.V. Club}}</ref>

Despite receiving positive responses for the season three premiere,<ref>{{cite web |date=October 8, 2014 |title=Arrow: Season 3 |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s03/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150610103428/http://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s03/ |archive-date=June 10, 2015 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |work=rottentomatoes.com}}</ref> the second half of the season was met with criticism. The flashback sequences were characterized as sporadic and "superfluous", with Ra's al Ghul described as a "shallow" and "underutilized" villain "absent of clear antagonism",<ref>{{cite web |author=Fitzpatrick, Kevin |date=May 15, 2015 |title=Where 'Arrow' Season 3 Went Wrong |url=https://screencrush.com/arrow-season-3-review-what-went-wrong/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161223041927/http://screencrush.com/arrow-season-3-review-what-went-wrong/ |archive-date=December 23, 2016 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |work=ScreenCrush}}</ref> although Matt Nable was generally praised for his portrayal of the character. Furthermore, while parallels to [[Batman]] had always existed in the show, the use of such a major character from Batman's rogues gallery and the essential application of the "Daughter of the Demon" and several other Batman and Ra's al Ghul storylines applied to Queen came under particular fire from viewers, who accused the show of "ripping off" Batman.<ref>{{cite web |last=McKalin |first=Vamien |date=March 1, 2015 |title='Arrow' TV Series: Is It Just A Huge Batman Rip-Off? Yeah It Is |url=http://www.techtimes.com/articles/36373/20150301/arrow-tv-series-is-it-just-a-huge-batman-rip-off-yeah-it-is.htm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170122132105/http://www.techtimes.com/articles/36373/20150301/arrow-tv-series-is-it-just-a-huge-batman-rip-off-yeah-it-is.htm |archive-date=January 22, 2017 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |work=Tech Times}}</ref> The season finale was described as "dull", "lacking scope", and "underwhelming" by ''[[IGN]]''<nowiki/>'s Jesse Schedeen in light of the "high standard" the show had previously established for its finales. He cemented the mixed reception of season three as being "haphazardly paced" and "struggling to develop a clear sense of direction".<ref>{{cite web |author=Jesse Schedeen |date=May 20, 2015 |title=Arrow: Season 3 Review |url=http://au.ign.com/articles/2015/05/20/arrow-season-3-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171202180949/http://au.ign.com/articles/2015/05/20/arrow-season-3-review |archive-date=December 2, 2017 |access-date=January 1, 2016 |work=IGN}}</ref> The third season holds a score of 89% on Rotten Tomatoes based on 9 reviews, with an average rating of 8.37/10. The site's consensus reads, "''Arrow'' stays on target with new characters and a steady supply of exciting action."<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow: Season 3 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s03 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171129070101/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s03/ |archive-date=November 29, 2017 |access-date=May 4, 2020 |website=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref>

The fourth season received mixed reviews. The season earned praise for the action scenes and [[Neal McDonough]]'s performance as Damien Darhk. However, it also received increasingly negative reviews for its mundane flashbacks, lack of narrative focus, and formulaic season finale.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=June 2, 2016 |title=Arrow: Season 4 Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2016/06/02/arrow-season-4-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160625173926/http://www.ign.com/articles/2016/06/02/arrow-season-4-review |archive-date=June 25, 2016 |access-date=June 26, 2016 |website=IGN}}</ref> Ryan Fleming of ''Deadbeatspanel.com'' noted that ''Arrow'' was "honoring the comics, but it isn't beholden to them. Characters ... have been introduced, but they aren't exact replicas of their comic counterparts. Instead, the characters tend to be loosely connected."<ref name="DBP">{{cite web |last1=Fleming |first1=Ryan |date=September 6, 2016 |title=66 TV Shows Based on Comics in the Works |url=http://www.deadbeatspanel.com/tv-shows-based-on-comics/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161022164635/http://www.deadbeatspanel.com/tv-shows-based-on-comics/ |archive-date=October 22, 2016 |access-date=October 22, 2016 |website=Deadbeatspanel.com}}</ref> Lesley Goldberg of ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'' noted the presence of the character Thea "Speedy" Queen as one of the larger departures from the comics in the series, as well as the character's early willingness to kill.<ref name="HR1">{{cite web |last1=Goldberg |first1=Lesley |date=October 3, 2012 |title=How Does The CW's 'Arrow' Compare to the DC Series? A Comic Book Expert Weighs In |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/cw-green-arrow-tv-show-dc-comic-book-374921 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161012050837/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/cw-green-arrow-tv-show-dc-comic-book-374921 |archive-date=October 12, 2016 |access-date=October 22, 2016 |work=The Hollywood Reporter}}</ref> [[Comic Book Resources]]' Kevin Melrose has also noted the series tendency to have loose connections to the source material.<ref name="CBR1">{{cite web |last1=Melrose |first1=Kevin |date=February 8, 2012 |title=Arrow Is 'Sophisticated and Edgy,' and a Little Like the Bourne Series |url=http://www.cbr.com/arrow-is-sophisticated-and-edgy-and-a-little-like-the-bourne-series/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161022155642/http://www.cbr.com/arrow-is-sophisticated-and-edgy-and-a-little-like-the-bourne-series/ |archive-date=October 22, 2016 |access-date=October 22, 2016 |work=Comic Book Resources}}</ref> Rotten Tomatoes gave the season an 85% approval rating based on 10 reviews, with an average rating of 7.55/10. The critical consensus reads: "Season four of ''Arrow'' flourishes with a refreshing new tone, a thrilling new villain, and a gripping story arc."<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow: Season 4 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s04 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401100536/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s04 |archive-date=April 1, 2019 |access-date=November 25, 2018 |publisher=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref>

The fifth season received mostly positive reviews from critics, with praise directed towards the performances of Amell and [[Josh Segarra]], action sequences, storytelling, and the season finale.<ref>{{cite web |last=Wilkins |first=Alasdair |date=March 30, 2017 |title=Arrow is great once more, with a little help from his friends |url=https://www.avclub.com/arrow-is-great-once-more-with-a-little-help-from-his-f-1798190862 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614044741/https://tv.avclub.com/arrow-is-great-once-more-with-a-little-help-from-his-f-1798190862 |archive-date=June 14, 2018 |access-date=May 11, 2018 |website=[[The A.V. Club]]}}</ref> ''IGN'' gave the season a score of 8.7 out of 10, stating that it "managed to overcome them and recapture a lot of what made the show so memorable in its first two seasons."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=June 1, 2017 |title=Arrow: Season 5 Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2017/06/01/arrow-season-5-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170606051827/http://www.ign.com/articles/2017/06/01/arrow-season-5-review |archive-date=June 6, 2017 |access-date=June 6, 2017 |website=IGN}}</ref> Rotten Tomatoes reported an approval rating of 88% based on 13 reviews, with an average rating of 7.38/10. The site's consensus reads, "No stranger to dramatic twists and turns, season five of ''Arrow'' continues to introduce new villains and surprise viewers despite some inconsistency".<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow: Season 5 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s05 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171205105246/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s05/ |archive-date=December 5, 2017 |access-date=November 25, 2018 |publisher=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref>

The sixth season received mixed reviews from critics. ''IGN'' gave the season a score of 6.7 out of 10, stating that it "captured the show at its best and worst, with a strong finish redeeming months of disappointment."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=May 25, 2018 |title=Arrow: Season 6 Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2018/05/25/arrow-season-6-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614045538/http://www.ign.com/articles/2018/05/25/arrow-season-6-review |archive-date=June 14, 2018 |access-date=June 14, 2018 |website=IGN}}</ref> Rotten Tomatoes reported an approval rating of 64% based on 7 reviews, and the average score is 6.86/10. The site's consensus reads, "''Arrow''{{'}}s sixth season deals with the literal fallout from the explosion in season five's finale and promises a drastic change in direction for the series".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Arrow: Season 6 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s06/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191022131212/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s06 |archive-date=October 22, 2019 |access-date=December 10, 2019 |publisher=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref>

The seventh season received more favorable reviews than the previous season. ''IGN'' gave the season a score of 7.4 out of 10, crediting Beth Schwartz's work with giving new life and energy to the show, while remarking that the season was "full of missed potential". Particular praise was given to Amell and Rickards' performances, as well as new directions for the show via "more willingness to take risks and venture off the beaten path this year, even if it often bit off more than it could chew with its large ensemble cast."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=May 29, 2019 |title=Arrow: Season 7 Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2019/05/29/arrow-season-7-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190530015137/https://www.ign.com/articles/2019/05/29/arrow-season-7-review |archive-date=May 30, 2019 |access-date=June 22, 2019 |website=IGN}}</ref> Rotten Tomatoes reported an approval rating of 88% based on 211 reviews, with an average score of 7.35/10.<ref>{{cite web |title=Arrow: Season 7 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s07/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190523201233/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s07 |archive-date=May 23, 2019 |access-date=November 7, 2019 |publisher=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref>

The eighth and final season was met with an approval rating of 95% on Rotten Tomatoes based on 125 reviews, with an average score of 7.5/10. The site's consensus reads, "Oliver Queen's final adventure hits emotional peaks while spearheading the game-changing 'Crisis on Infinite Earths', giving the Emerald Archer a rousing sendoff."<ref>{{Cite web |title=Arrow: Season 8 – Rotten Tomatoes |url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/arrow/s08 |access-date=November 7, 2022 |publisher=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]}}</ref>

